From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH] mpegpsdemux: do not adjust base_time against the upstream segment

On the first pack header, base_time is set to the first SCR - the sender's own
clock - and gst_ps_demux_send_segment() adds it to the segment it pushes, so
that the segment and the buffer timestamps (which come straight from the PES
clock) share one timeline. A block right after that then "adjusts" base_time
against the upstream segment:

    if (demux->sink_segment.start > demux->base_time)
      diff = -(demux->sink_segment.start - demux->base_time);
    else
      diff = demux->base_time - demux->sink_segment.start;
    if (diff > GST_SECOND) {
      demux->base_time += diff;
    }

For any live stream - an RTP MPEG-PS feed arriving through rtpmp2pdepay, where
upstream's segment starts at zero and the SCR is a wall clock - the second arm
gives diff == base_time, the comparison passes, and the addition leaves
base_time at twice the sender's clock. The segment then starts tens of
thousands of seconds away from the buffers that follow it. Nothing downstream
can reconcile the two: vacompositor sees frames whose running time is hours
away, holds them, and holds their VA surfaces with them until the decoder's
pool has taken the whole GPU.

The adjustment cannot be repaired by fixing the arithmetic, because moving
base_time moves only the segment. Buffer timestamps are assigned from the PES
clock in gst_ps_demux_send_data() and are not touched, so any base_time other
than the one the segment arithmetic already assumes leaves the two
disagreeing. Keeping base_time at the first SCR is what the untaken branch
does, and it is the branch that produces a consistent timeline - so the block
goes.

**What this tree looked like before 2026-09-16.** This patch used to be
written against a locally modified copy of the block rather than against
upstream: 0002 had flipped the sign of the first arm, wrapped the comparison
in abs(), and made the adjustment a subtraction. That version fails
differently - the subtraction sets base_time to exactly zero rather than
doubling it - and it fails intermittently, because diff is a gint64 while
abs() takes an int, so the value is truncated to its low 32 bits before the
comparison:

    base_time 10:56:45.047 -> 39405047488888 ns -> (int) -1277451912 -> runs
    base_time 11:50:19.473 -> 42619473455555 ns -> (int)   512977347 -> skipped

Two live streams from the same sender, differing only in where their clocks
happened to be, took opposite branches. GCC does say so with
-Wabsolute-value, which is not in the build's warning set. That modification
has been removed from 0002 and this patch regenerated against upstream. The
block was deleted either way, so the built source is unchanged but for the
wording of the comment left in its place, which described the subtraction.

The SCR bookkeeping it sat inside is untouched: first_scr, first_scr_offset and
the initial SCR rate are still recorded, and seeking, which reads base_time
back through first_pts, still sees the value the segment was built from.

--- a/_p2_/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
+++ b/_p2_/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
@@ -1866,8 +1866,6 @@
 
   /* keep the first src in order to calculate delta time */
   if (G_UNLIKELY (demux->first_scr == G_MAXUINT64)) {
-    gint64 diff;
-
     demux->first_scr = scr;
     demux->first_scr_offset = demux->cur_scr_offset;
     demux->base_time = MPEGTIME_TO_GSTTIME (demux->first_scr);
@@ -1876,19 +1874,12 @@
     /* at begin consider the new_rate as the scr rate, bytes/clock ticks */
     scr_rate_n = new_rate;
     scr_rate_d = CLOCK_FREQ;
-    /* our SCR timeline might have offset wrt upstream timeline */
-    if (demux->sink_segment.format == GST_FORMAT_TIME) {
-      if (demux->sink_segment.start > demux->base_time)
-        diff = -(demux->sink_segment.start - demux->base_time);
-      else
-        diff = demux->base_time - demux->sink_segment.start;
-      if (diff > GST_SECOND) {
-        GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (demux, "diff of %" GST_TIME_FORMAT
-            " wrt upstream start %" GST_TIME_FORMAT "; adjusting base",
-            GST_TIME_ARGS (diff), GST_TIME_ARGS (demux->sink_segment.start));
-        demux->base_time += diff;
-      }
-    }
+    /* Local fix: what used to be here adjusted base_time against the upstream
+     * segment. For a live feed that segment starts at zero while base_time is
+     * the sender's own clock, so the adjustment came out as base_time +=
+     * base_time. See the patch header for why it cannot be corrected in place:
+     * base_time has to stay the value the segment arithmetic in
+     * gst_ps_demux_send_segment() is written against. */
   } else if (G_LIKELY (demux->first_scr_offset != demux->cur_scr_offset)) {
     /* estimate byte rate related to the SCR */
     scr_rate_n = demux->cur_scr_offset - demux->first_scr_offset;
